  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical findings are limited to a single case study of Warsaw, which may not fully represent the diverse urban trajectories of other post-socialist cities in the region.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the spatial distribution of traffic noise exposure across different socio-spatial neighborhoods in Warsaw, Poland. By combining spatial regression models with neighborhood segmentation analysis, the research maps how post-socialist urban legacies and modern development patterns interact to shape environmental inequalities.",
      "why": "It extends environmental justice research beyond its typical North American and Western European focus into the Central and Eastern European context, demonstrating how a city's specific post-socialist transition influences modern exposure to environmental hazards."
